⚠️ **IMPORTANT**: Hidden field values are restored by array index, so adding, removing, or reordering fields still wipes them — or copies a secret into the wrong field.

<details>
<summary>Details and fix</summary>

`originalHiddenFieldValues` is indexed against the *original* field array, but after `CipherExport.toView` the array comes from the request, where the positions may differ:

- Original `[Text "note", Hidden "secret"]`, user deletes `"note"` → incoming `[Hidden "secret" (null)]`. Index 0 in the original was a Text field (`undefined`), so no restore happens and the hidden value is wiped — the original bug.
- Original `[Hidden "a"=1, Hidden "b"=2]`, user reorders → `"b"` receives `1` and `"a"` receives `2`. Two hidden values are silently swapped, and the user cannot see the corruption.

Matching by name and only restoring when the incoming value was redacted avoids both:

```ts
const originalHiddenFieldValues = new Map<string, string>(
  (cipherView.fields ?? [])
    .filter((f) => f.type === FieldType.Hidden)
    .map((f) => [f.name, f.value]),
);

cipherView = CipherExport.toView(req, cipherView);

if (!canViewPassword) {
  cipherView.fields?.forEach((field) => {
    if (
      field.type === FieldType.Hidden &&
      field.value == null &&
      originalHiddenFieldValues.has(field.name)
    ) {
      field.value = originalHiddenFieldValues.get(field.name);
    }
  });
}
```

</details>

